  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1226 PM EST Fri Nov 20 2026\n\nSurface high pressure centered over the Appalachian Mountains \ncontinues to provide tranquil, dry weather for the next 48 hours or \nso. Our mild weather pattern persists. High temperatures have been \n15-20 degrees above normals and lows about 5-10 degrees above normal \nthe past few days. With southerly low level flow and WAA, \ntemperatures are expected to remain in the mid to upper 60s for \nhighs today and Saturday. This is abnormal for this time of year, as \ndaily records for today (November 20th) and Saturday (November 21st) \nare around 70 degrees! \n\nAs high pressure gets pushed eastward Saturday due to influence from \na trough over the Four Corners region, rain chances return on Sunday \nas a cold front approaches. A low pressure system will traverse the \nUpper Midwest, with a trailing attendant cold front sweeping through \nthe Great Lakes. There are 60-80% chances for rain, however, \nthe day will not be a washout. Scattered, light showers will \nmove through the area at times on Sunday during the day. \nRainfall amounts are expected to be light (up to 0.10\") as \nshowers outrun the better low level moisture and lift with the \nactual front. It will be breezy on Sunday with a tightening \npressure gradient in the vicinity of the front; Wind gusts will \nbe between 20-25 mph with a shift in direction from southwest to\nnorthwest. With the rain chances primarily in the afternoon and\nevening, there should be sufficient diurnal heating for \ntemperatures to climb into the low to mid 60s for highs on \nSunday, especially in NW Ohio.\n\nFollowing the frontal passage, CAA and northerly flow ensues with \ntemperatures moderating back towards seasonable levels with highs in \nthe upper 30s to low 40s. As a high pressure system spills into the \nGreat Lakes region from central Canada, the forecast is leaning \ncooler and dry for early into middle part of next week. The GFS and \nECMWF are in solid agreement next week of 850 mb temps below 0C \nthrough the early part of next week. This should result in highs in \nthe low 40s and lows in the mid to upper 20s for several days. Given \nthat a strong El Nino is ongoing, a more active pattern may develop \nacross the southeastern US with the jet stream keeping activity \nsuppressed to the south. There is some indication in the long \nrange guidance of a 'Miller A' type system developing mid to \nlate next week, but a lot of uncertainty remains. It is \nplausible we get some scattered rain/snow showers at times by \nmid to late next week, but there are no real signs of winter \nyet. An early look at Thanksgiving has highs near 40 degrees \nwith 20-30% chances for scattered rain/snow showers.",
